Imports:
  - Types:
      - LocalTrade
    From: freqtrade/persistence
  - Types:
      - timeframe_to_minutes
    From: freqtrade/exchange

Usages:
  extension_point: |
    To add a new protection, subclass `IProtection`, set has_global_stop/has_local_stop,
    implement short_desc, global_stop, and stop_per_pair, then register the class name in
    the user's protections config list. ProtectionResolver (in freqtrade/resolvers)
    dynamically loads the named class at runtime — this cell only defines the contract.

Annotations: |
  This cell is freqtrade's risk-control extension point: handlers that can temporarily lock
  trading globally or per-pair based on recent trade history (drawdown, consecutive losses,
  low-profit streaks, cooldowns).

  Use `extension_point` to implement a new handler. Use `timeframe_to_minutes` from Imports
  when a protection's lookback window is expressed in candles rather than wall-clock time.

---

"ProtectionReturn(lock: bool, until: datetime, reason: str | None, lock_side: str)":
  location: iprotection.py
  annotations: |
    Result of a protection check: whether to lock, until when, why, and which side (long/
    short/"*") the lock applies to.

"IProtection(config: dict[str, Any], protection_config: dict[str, Any])":
  location: iprotection.py
  annotations: |
    Abstract base for one risk-control handler, consulted by ProtectionManager after every
    trade close.
  properties:
    "name -> str": |
      Class name of the handler, used in logs and API responses.
  methods:
    "short_desc() -> text:str": |
      One-line description including this handler's configured parameters.

      Constraints:
      - Abstract: every concrete handler must override this
    "global_stop(date_now: datetime, side: str, starting_balance: float) -> result:ProtectionReturn | None": |
      Decide whether trading should be locked globally (all pairs) as of `date_now`.

      `side`: "long" or "short"
      `result`: `ProtectionReturn` to apply a lock, or `None` for no action

      Constraints:
      - Abstract; only called if has_global_stop is True
    "stop_per_pair(pair: str, date_now: datetime, side: str, starting_balance: float) -> result:ProtectionReturn | None": |
      Decide whether trading should be locked for `pair` specifically as of `date_now`.

      Constraints:
      - Abstract; only called if has_local_stop is True
    "calculate_lock_end(trades: list[LocalTrade]) -> until:datetime": |
      Compute the lock expiry timestamp from the most recent of `trades`' close times plus
      this handler's configured stop duration.

"IProtection::StoplossGuard()":
  location: stoploss_guard.py
  annotations: |
    Locks trading (globally or per-pair) after too many stoplossed trades within a lookback
    window. One of several concrete handlers in this cell; see `extension_point`.

